WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.450
Some routes must visit every place exactly once.

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:07.340
A move can be perfectly legal now and still ruin the

00:00:07.340 --> 00:00:09.920
route later, by cutting off a place you still

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:10.690
need to visit.

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:14.430
Good solvers look ahead at what each move leaves behind.

00:00:15.470 --> 00:00:18.350
Without looking ahead you find out about a mistake only

00:00:18.350 --> 00:00:19.320
at the very end.

00:00:19.690 --> 00:00:22.370
Checking what remains after each move catches dead ends

00:00:22.370 --> 00:00:24.280
early and saves most of the work.

00:00:25.070 --> 00:00:27.640
A token moves on a three by three grid of dots,

00:00:27.990 --> 00:00:30.580
stepping to a neighbour up, down, left or right.

00:00:30.950 --> 00:00:33.200
It must visit every dot exactly once.

00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:36.400
Starting in a corner, can it finish in the centre?

00:00:36.650 --> 00:00:38.320
Pause and try to trace it.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:42.990
A spiral works.

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:46.850
Go along the top, down the right side, along the bottom,

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:48.810
up the left, and step into the centre.

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:51.940
Every dot is visited once, and the path ends

00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:52.670
in the centre.

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:56.440
Now ask a harder question: can a path start in the

00:00:56.440 --> 00:00:57.290
middle of a side?

00:00:57.660 --> 00:00:59.230
Colour the dots like a chessboard.

00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:02.590
The corners and the centre are dark: five dots.

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:05.450
The middles of the sides are light: four dots.

00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:07.270
Every step changes colour.

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:10.880
A path through nine dots that starts on a light dot

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:14.250
would visit light, dark, light, dark, and so on:

00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:16.550
five light dots and four dark.

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:19.030
But there are only four light dots.

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:20.830
So no such path exists.

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:24.210
One simple count rules out every attempt at once.

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:26.790
Here is another look-ahead rule.

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:30.400
A dot with only one connection can only be the first or

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:32.740
the last dot of the route, because you cannot

00:01:32.740 --> 00:01:33.710
pass through it.

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:36.920
If there are three such dots, no route can

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:37.690
visit them all.

00:01:38.470 --> 00:01:41.470
After each move, check that everything unvisited can

00:01:41.470 --> 00:01:42.300
still be reached.

00:01:42.950 --> 00:01:44.280
Dead ends must be endpoints.

00:01:44.590 --> 00:01:46.320
And colouring can prove impossibility.

00:01:47.330 --> 00:01:50.530
Notebook challenge: on a grid of dots with two rows and

00:01:50.530 --> 00:01:53.530
three columns, can a path with only horizontal and

00:01:53.530 --> 00:01:57.050
vertical steps visit every dot once, starting at one end

00:01:57.050 --> 00:01:59.340
of the top row and finishing at the other?
